From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: wait for a running IRQ handler before
 resetting a core

rocket_reset() calls drm_sched_stop(), which stops the scheduler and
returns.  It does not wait for a threaded handler that is already
running, so the comment that followed it -- "Remaining interrupts have
been handled" -- stated an assumption rather than something the code
arranged.  A completion handler that is mid-flight when the reset starts
keeps running against a core the reset is about to put through
rocket_core_reset(), and against an in_flight_job the reset has already
dropped.

Call synchronize_irq(core->irq) after drm_sched_stop() and reword the
comment to say what holds afterwards.

It has to go before the scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) rather than
inside it.  rocket_job_handle_irq() takes job_lock -- 0027 moved the
register writes under it, so it now holds that lock for the whole
handler -- and waiting for the handler while holding the lock it wants
would deadlock instead of fencing anything.  Nothing is held at that
point, and both callers, rocket_job_timedout() and rocket_reset_work(),
run in process context, so sleeping there is allowed.

The other way into rocket_job_handle_irq() on this SoC is the completion
poll work, which the hrtimer_cancel()/cancel_work_sync() pair above
already fences; this closes the shared interrupt thread, which the
RK3576 still takes for the DMA-error bits.

This does not stop a handler that has already read in_flight_job from
finishing its work on the job the reset is about to drop.  That window
needs the check and the register writes to be one step under the lock,
which is what 0027 does; the two are complementary.

Igor Paunovic's observation on linux-rockchip; the function is shared
with the RK3588, which carries the same fix as rocket/091.

  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-rockchip/20260817113603.1436067-3-gahing@gahingwoo.com/

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 17 ++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 50a76b83a..37fa9caac 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -787,9 +787,20 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 	drm_sched_stop(&core->sched, bad);
 
 	/*
-	 * Remaining interrupts have been handled, but we might still have
-	 * stuck jobs. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
-	 * manually calling pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * drm_sched_stop() returns without waiting for a threaded handler that
+	 * is already running, so wait for one here.  This has to stay outside
+	 * job_lock: rocket_job_handle_irq() takes that lock, so waiting for the
+	 * handler while holding it would be waiting for a handler that is
+	 * waiting for us.  Nothing is held at this point, and both callers run
+	 * in process context, so sleeping here is allowed.  The polled
+	 * completion is already fenced above by cancel_work_sync().
+	 */
+	synchronize_irq(core->irq);
+
+	/*
+	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
+	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
+	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
-- 
